BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Zak Jones in Conversation with Jean Marc Ah-Sen
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260521T203000
UID:bm-5161520260521@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nJoin us for a conversation with Zak Jones\, author
  of\nFancy Gap.\nMeet the author\, mingle\, and get your book signed!\nZak
  Jones\nis a writer\, literary scholar and US Army Veteran. A dual Canadia
 n-American citizen\, he grew up between rural North Carolina and Toronto. 
 His short story\, "So Much More to Say\," won the Writers’ Trust of Cana
 da’s 2023 Bronwen Wallace Award for Emerging Writers\, and his story\, "
 Love Handles\," won the 2023 Norma Epstein National Award. Zak holds an MA
  in English Literature & Creative Writing from the University of Toronto\,
  where he is currently a Ph.D. candidate studying veterans’ narratives i
 n American Literature.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Restaurant Kid
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260526T200000
UID:bm-5072020260526@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[Off-site]\nThe Toronto Public Library presents Rachel Phan at
  TPL's Yorkville Branch!\nRachel Phan\nis a Chinese Canadian author. A gra
 duate of Toronto Metropolitan University’s Master of Journalism program\
 , she and her work have been featured on CBC\, Huffpost\, the\nWall Street
  Journal\, the Washington Post\,\nthe\nToronto Star\,\nthe\nNational Post\
 , Chatelaine\,\nand\nMaclean’s\n. Her instant national bestselling book\
 ,\nRestaurant Kid: A Memoir of Family and Belonging\n(2025)\, was named on
 e of CBC Books’ Best Books of 2025 and longlisted for Canada Reads in 20
 26. She lives in Toronto\, ON.
LOCATION:22 Yorkville Ave\, TPL Yorkville Branch
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Threads\, Objects\, Storytelling
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260528T203000
UID:bm-5269820260528@shop.queenbooks.ca
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/8100306/att.KeGn9
 4akg21xdOkMc3g_yoGvGtOQP3PmsKcqz28v_qQ.JPG?cb=1778174181
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nPlease join Alex and Diana for an evening of conve
 rsation. Both authors will read from their books\, discuss the inspiration
 s they have shared over the past decade\, and take questions from the audi
 ence before signing books. Alex will share a writing prompt and Diana will
  have vintage textiles on hand for those interested in starting a stitchin
 g project. Their hope is that guests will walk out into the night with a s
 eed of an idea or approach to a creative act (writing\, making\, activism)
  and a feeling of togetherness.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:STORYTIME: Let's Eat and Bollywood Beat!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260607T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260607T113000
UID:bm-5469220260607@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nBring your little ones for a storytime of Let's Ea
 t and Bollywood Beat! with authors Rina Singh and Anita Yasuda!\nThis eve
 nt is free\, but you may RSVP through\nEventbrite\n.\nRina Singh\nis a chi
 ldren’s author whose curiosity and love for storytelling shape the heart
  of her books. Reflecting her interest in real-life events and the untold 
 stories they hold\, her writing spans themes of environmental justice\, so
 cial change and the magic woven into everyday life. Her critically acclaim
 ed titles include\nGrandmother School\n\, winner of the Christie Harris Il
 lustrated Children’s Literature Prize\;\nDiwali: A Festival of Lights\n\
 , nominated for the Red Cedar Award\;\nOnce\, a Bird\n\, selected for IBBY
  Canada's Silent Books Collection\; and\nBarefoot Skateboarders\n. She liv
 es in Toronto.\nAnita Yasuda\nis the author of\nUp\, Up\, Ever Up! Junko T
 abei: A Life in the Mountains\, Diwali: A Festival of Joy\nand\nBollywood 
 Beat!\n\, as well as many other books for young readers. She has received 
 a Boston Globe-Horn Book nonfiction honor\, a Freeman Book Award of Note\,
  a Junior Library Guild Gold Standard selection and a nomination for the F
 orest of Reading Yellow Cedar Award.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Mother Joins the Resistance
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260610T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260610T203000
UID:bm-5469320260610@shop.queenbooks.ca
DESCRIPTION:[In Store]\nJoin us for the launch of\nMy Mother Joins the Res
 istance\nby Richard Harrison! Meet the author\, mingle\, and get your book
  signed.\nRichard Harrison\nis the winner of the 2017 Governor General’s
  Award for Poetry for\nOn Not Losing My Father’s Ashes in the Flood.\nRi
 chard’s writings have been published across Canada as well as in the Uni
 ted States\, England and Italy. His individual poems\, and two books\, hav
 e been translated into six languages including Spanish\, Farsi\, French an
 d Arabic. This year he was honoured with the Writers’ Guild of Alberta
 ’s Golden Pen for Lifetime Achievement. Professor Emeritus of English at
  Mount Royal University\, currently Richard is a full-time poet\, essayist
 \, editor and writing mentor in Calgary\, where he lives with his wife\, L
 isa.
LOCATION:914 Queen Street East
URL:https://shop.queenbooks.ca/events
X-SOURCE:Queen Books
END:VEVENT
